<chapter xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="notes-graphs" component="graphs">
  <title>Graph Theory</title>
  <handout xml:id="graphs-notation-term" component="graphs-notation-term">
    <title>Notation and Terminology</title>
    <page>
      <p>
        <alert>Peer instruction questions 1<ndash/>3.</alert>
      </p>
      <image source="spanning_subgraph" width="60%">
        <description component="a"><p>Two graphs</p></description>
      </image>
      <p component="a">
        We have that <m>\bfH</m> is a spanning subgraph of <m>\bfG</m> because all vertices of <m>\bfG</m> appear in <m>\bfH</m>.
      </p>
      <image source="non_subgraph" width="60%">
        <description component="a"><p>Two graphs</p></description>
      </image>
      <p component="a">
        We have that <m>\bfH</m> is not a subgraph of <m>\bfG</m> because the edge <m>\set{8,10}</m> in <m>\bfH</m> is not an edge in <m>\bfG</m>.
      </p>
      <image source="induced_subgraph" width="60%">
        <description component="a"><p>Two graphs</p></description>
      </image>
      <p component="a">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              We see that <m>\bfH</m> is an induced subgraph of <m>\bfG</m> because every edge of <m>\bfG</m> between vertices that are in <m>\bfH</m> is also an edge of <m>\bfH</m>. 
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The word <q>vertex</q> is singular. Its plural is <q>vertices</q>. Yeah, Latin gave this to us, much like it gave us <q>matrix</q> and <q>matrices</q>. 
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Formally, <m>\bfG=(V,E)</m> is an ordered pair of sets. Moreover, <m>E</m> is a set of sets with each element of <m>E</m> being a 2-element subset of <m>V</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </page>
    <page>
      <activity>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Suppose <m>\bfH</m> is an induced spanning subgraph of a graph <m>\bfG</m>. Discuss with your group what this would mean.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            Here we must have that <m>\bfG</m> and <m>\bfH</m> are the same graph. The spanning property implies that <m>\bfH</m> has all of the vertices of <m>\bfG</m>. Being induced then forces all of the edges of <m>\bfG</m> to be in <m>\bfH</m> since <m>\bfH</m> contains all edges of <m>\bfG</m> that have both their endpoints in <m>\bfH</m>, which has all of the vertices of <m>\bfG</m>.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </activity>
    </page>
    <page>
      <p>
        <alert>Peer instruction questions 4<ndash/>5.</alert>
      </p>
      <image source="isomorphic_graphs-PI" width="65%">
        <description component="a"><p>Two graphs</p></description>
      </image>
      <p component="a">
        These graphs are isomorphic. One possible isomorphism is shown below.
      </p>
      <tabular component="a">
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell right="minor"><m>v</m></cell>
          <cell><m>a</m></cell>
          <cell><m>b</m></cell>
          <cell><m>c</m></cell>
          <cell><m>d</m></cell>
          <cell><m>e</m></cell>
          <cell><m>f</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell right="minor"><m>f(v)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>1</m></cell>
          <cell><m>3</m></cell>
          <cell><m>5</m></cell>
          <cell><m>6</m></cell>
          <cell><m>2</m></cell>
          <cell><m>4</m></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
      <image source="nonisomorphic_graphs-PI" width="65%">
        <description component="a"><p>Two graphs</p></description>
      </image>
      <p component="a">
        These graphs are not isomorphic. To see why they are not, we try to construct an isomorphism. Since <m>a</m> and <m>1</m> are the only vertices of degree <m>1</m>, any isomorphism would have to pair them. This then forces <m>c</m> and <m>2</m>, as the unique neighbors of the vertices of degree <m>1</m> to be paired. However, all neighbors of <m>c</m> have degree <m>3</m>, while <m>2</m> has a neighbor (<m>4</m>) of degree <m>2</m>. Another independent reason would be that <m>f</m> and <m>4</m> are the unique vertices of degree <m>2</m> in these graphs, so they would have to be paired. However, <m>f</m> has two neighbors of degree <m>3</m>, while <m>4</m> has a neighbor of degree <m>4</m>. As a third independent reason, notice that in the left graph, the vertex of degree <m>1</m> and the vertex of degree <m>2</m> are distance 3 apart. However, in the right graph, the vertex of degree <m>1</m> and the vertex of degree <m>2</m> are distance 2 apart.
      </p>
    </page>
    <page>
      <p workspace="3cm">
        <alert>On the Complexity of Graph Isomorphism</alert>
      </p>
      <p component="a">
        The question we would like to answer algorithmically is <q>Given two graphs <m>\bfG</m> and <m>\bfH</m>, is <m>\bfG</m> isomorphic to <m>\bfH</m>?</q> The two most-studied classes of computational problems are <m>\bfP</m> and <m>\bfNP</m>, with a focus on <m>\bfNP</m>-complete problems. The problems in <m>\bfP</m> have efficient algorithms, by which we roughly mean the number of operations an algorithm takes is polynomial in the size of the input (usually number of vertices for graphs). The <m>\bfNP</m>-complete problems are believed to be computationally intractable <em>but</em> must all be of a type where a yes or no answer can be readily verified. (For instance, if I give you a function that I claim is an isomorphism, it's fast to verify this fact.) Most problems that fit this framework have been shown to be in <m>\bfP</m> or to be <m>\bfNP</m>-complete. However, graph isomorphism has not shown to be either. In fact, it is often considered to be the leading candidate for a problem that is neither in <m>\bfP</m> nor <m>\bfNP</m>-complete.
      </p>
      <theorem xml:id="thm-first-thm-gt">
        <title component="b">The First Theorem of Graph Theory</title>
        <title component="a">The First Theorem of Graph Theory aka The Handshaking Lemma</title>
        <statement>
          <p workspace="3cm">
            Let <m>\deg_{\bfG}(v)</m> denote the degree of vertex <m>v</m> in graph <m>\GVE</m>. Then
            <me>
              \sum_{v\in V}\deg_{\bfG}(v) = 2|E|
            </me>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <proof component="a">
          <p>
            The summation counts an edge <m>xy</m> twice: once when <m>v=x</m> and once when <m>v=y</m>.
          </p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>
      <p compnent="a">
        <me>
          |E| = \frac{1}{2} \sum_{v\in V}\deg_{\bfG}(v)
        </me>
      </p>
      <corollary xml:id="cor-num-odd-vts">
        <statement>
          <p workspace="1cm">
            In any graph, the number of vertices of odd degree is even.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <proof component="a">
          <p>
            Adding up an odd number of odd numbers gives an odd number, and the sum would remain odd no matter how many even numbers are added in.
          </p>
        </proof>
      </corollary>
      <p>
        <dl>
          <li>
            <title>Walk</title>
            <p workspace="1cm" component="b">
              
            </p>
            <p component="a">
              <m>(x_1,x_2,\dots,x_t)</m> so that <m>x_ix_{i+1}\in E</m> for <m>i=1,2,\dots,t-1</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Path</title>
            <p workspace="1cm" component="b">
              
            </p>
            <p component="a">
              Walk with no repeated vertices.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Cycle</title>
            <p workspace="1cm" component="b">
              
            </p>
            <p component="a">
              Path with <m>t\geq 3</m> for which <m>x_1x_t</m> is also an edge.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </page>
    <page>
      <definition xml:id="def-tree">
        <statement>
          <p workspace="1cm" component="b">
            <ul>
              <li>A <term>tree</term> is </li>
              <li>A vertex <m>v</m> in a tree <m>\GVE</m> is called a <term>leaf</term> provided that</li>
            </ul>
          </p>
          <p workspace="1cm" component="a">
            <ul>
              <li>A <term>tree</term> is a connected graph with no cycles.</li>
              <li>A vertex <m>v</m> in a tree <m>\GVE</m> is called a <term>leaf</term> provided that <m>\deg_\bfG(v) = 1</m>.</li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>
      <theorem xml:id="thm-tree-leaves">
        <statement>
          <p workspace="5cm">
            Every tree on at least two vertices has at least two leaves.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </theorem>
      <theorem xml:id="thm-tree-unique-path">
        <statement>
          <p workspace="7cm">
            If <m>\bfT</m> is a tree, then for every pair of distinct vertices <m>u,v</m>, there exists a unique path from <m>u</m> to <m>v</m> in <m>\bfT</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </theorem>
    </page>
    <page>
      <activity>
        <statement>
          <p workspace="6cm">
            <ol marker="a.">
              <li>With your neighbors, use mathematical induction to prove that every tree on <m>n</m> vertices has exactly <m>n-1</m> edges.
              </li>
              <li>
                How many edges would an <m>n</m>-vertex forest consisting of <m>k</m> trees have?
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <proof>
            <p>
              For the base case, when <m>n=1</m>, the tree has only one vertex and thus no edges. Since <m>n-1 = 1-1=0</m>, we have the desired relationship. Now assume that for some <m>k\geq 1</m>, every tree with <m>k</m> vertices has exactly <m>k-1</m> edges. Let <m>\bfT</m> be a tree with <m>k+1</m> vertices. The tree <m>\bfT</m> has a leaf <m>v</m>. Form <m>\bfT'</m> from <m>\bfT</m> by deleting <m>v</m> and its edge. Then <m>\bfT'</m> has <m>k</m> vertices, so by the induction hypothesis, <m>\bfT'</m> has <m>k-1</m> edges. However, <m>\bfT</m> has one more edge (incident with <m>v</m>) than <m>\bfT'</m>, so <m>\bfT</m> has <m>k = k+1-1</m> edges.
            </p>
          </proof>
          <p>
            Notice in the proof above that it was essential to delete a leaf, as otherwise we would not know that the new graph is still a tree (it might be a forest).
          </p>
          <p>
            A forest is a graph with no cycles, meaning that each component of a forest is itself a tree. Thus, we would need to have <m>n-k</m> edges.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </activity>
    </page>
  </handout>
  <handout xml:id="notes-eulerham" component="eulerham">
    <title>Eulerian and Hamiltonian Graphs</title>
    <p>Let <m>\bfG</m> be a graph without isolated vertices. We say that <m>\bfG</m> is <term>eulerian</term><idx>
      <h>graph</h>
      <h>eulerian</h>
    </idx> provided that there is a sequence <m>(x_0,x_1,x_2,\dots,x_t)</m> of
    vertices from <m>\bfG</m>, with repetition allowed, so that <ol>
      <li component="b"><m>x_0=x_t</m>;</li>
      <li component="a"><m>x_0=x_t</m>; (This means that the sequence starts and ends at the same place.)</li>
      <li component="b">for every <m>i=0,1,\dots, t-1</m>, <m>x_ix_{i+1}</m> is an edge of <m>\bfG</m>;</li>
      <li component="a">for every <m>i=0,1,\dots, t-1</m>, <m>x_ix_{i+1}</m> is an edge of <m>\bfG</m>; (This means that the sequence is a walk.)</li>
      <li component="b">for every edge <m>e\in E</m>, there is a unique integer <m>i</m> with <m>0\le i\lt t</m>
        for which <m>e=x_ix_{i+1}</m>.</li>
      <li component="a">for every edge <m>e\in E</m>, there is a unique integer <m>i</m> with <m>0\le i\lt t</m>
        for which <m>e=x_ix_{i+1}</m>. (This means every edge is used <em>exactly</em> once.)</li>  
      </ol>
    </p>
    <p>When <m>\bfG</m> is eulerian, a sequence satisfying these three conditions is called an <term>eulerian
      circuit</term><idx>
        <h>eulerian</h>
        <h>circuit</h>
      </idx>. A sequence of vertices <m>(x_0,x_1,\dots,x_t)</m> is called a <term>
        circuit</term><idx>
          <h>circuit</h>
        </idx> when it satisfies only the first two of these conditions.
      </p>
      <theorem xml:id="thm-eulerian">
        <statement>
          <p component="a">A graph <m>\bfG</m> is eulerian if and only if it is connected and every vertex has even
        degree. </p>
        <p component="b" workspace="1cm">A graph <m>\bfG</m> is eulerian if and only if </p>
        </statement>
      </theorem>
      
      <algorithm xml:id="alg-eulerian">
        <title>Eulerian Circuit Finder</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <dl>
              <li>
                <title>Input</title>
                <p>
                  A graph <m>\bfG=([n],E)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <title>Output</title>
                <p>
                  An eulerian circuit <m>C</m> in <m>\bfG</m>, a vertex of odd degree in <m>\bfG</m>, or a connected component of <m>\bfG</m> and an edge of <m>\bfG</m> that is not in that connected component.
                </p>
              </li>
            </dl>
          </p>
          <p>
            <ol marker="1.">
              <li>
                <p>
                  Initialize <m>C=(1)</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li xml:id="step-eulerian-subcircuit">
                <p>
                  While not every edge of <m>\bfG</m> is traversed, determine if any vertex of <m>C</m> is incident with an edge that has not been traversed. 
                  <ol marker="a.">
                    <li>
                      <p>
                        If all vertices of <m>C</m> have all their edges traversed, then return the vertices of <m>C</m> as a connected component of <m>\bfG</m> with an edge not traversed by <m>C</m> demonstrating that <m>\bfG</m> is not connected.
                      </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <p>
                        If <m>C</m> has a vertex incident with an edge that has not been traversed, call that vertex <m>u_0</m>. Construct a walk <m>W</m> starting from <m>u_0</m>. From vertex <m>u_i</m>, follow the edge traversed by neither <m>C</m> nor <m>W</m> going to the neighbor of <m>u_i</m> with smallest label.
                      </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <p>
                        The construction of <m>W</m> halts at a vertex <m>u_s</m> for which all edges have been traversed.
                        <ol marker="i.">
                          <li>
                            <p>
                              If <m>u_s\neq u_0</m>, then return <m>u_0</m> as a vertex of odd degree, showing that <m>\bfG</m> is not eulerian.
                            </p>
                          </li>
                          <li>
                            <p>
                              If <m>u_s = u_0</m>, update the circuit <m>C</m> by replacing <m>u_0</m> in <m>C</m> with the walk <m>W</m>. Continue iterating by returning to <xref ref="step-eulerian-subcircuit">step</xref>.
                            </p>
                          </li>
                        </ol>
                      </p>
                    </li>
                    
                  </ol> 
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Return <m>C</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </algorithm>
      <sidebyside widths="60% 35%">
        <image>
          <description component="a">
            <p>A graph with 14 vertices. The vertices are labeled with integers from 0 to 13. The neighbors of each vertex are specified in the table below.
            </p>
            <tabular>
              <row>
                <cell>Vertex</cell>
                <cell>Neighbors</cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                <cell><m>6</m>, <m>9</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>1</m></cell>
                <cell><m>4</m>, <m>5</m>, <m>9</m>, <m>13</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>2</m></cell>
                <cell><m>7</m>, <m>11</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>3</m></cell>
                <cell><m>4</m>, <m>5</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>4</m></cell>
                <cell><m>1</m>, <m>3</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>5</m></cell>
                <cell><m>1</m>, <m>3</m>, <m>6</m>, <m>8</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>6</m></cell>
                <cell><m>0</m>, <m>5</m>, <m>10</m>, <m>11</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>7</m></cell>
                <cell><m>2</m>, <m>8</m>, <m>11</m>, <m>13</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>8</m></cell>
                <cell><m>5</m>, <m>7</m>, <m>11</m>, <m>12</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>9</m></cell>
                <cell><m>0</m>, <m>1</m>, <m>11</m>, <m>12</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>10</m></cell>
                <cell><m>6</m>, <m>11</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>11</m></cell>
                <cell><m>2</m>, <m>6</m>, <m>7</m>, <m>8</m>, <m>9</m>, <m>10</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>12</m></cell>
                <cell><m>8</m>, <m>9</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>13</m></cell>
                <cell><m>1</m>, <m>7</m></cell>
              </row>
            </tabular>
          </description>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="euler-example-graph">
            <diagram dimensions="(450,300)" margins="5">
              <definition>graph={0:[6,9],1:[4,5,9,13],2:[7,11],3:[4,5],5:[6,8],6:[10,11],7:[8,11,13],8:[11,12],9:[11,12],10:[11]}</definition>
              <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,1,1)">
                <network graph="graph" scale="0.8" node-fill="#fcf" node-stroke="black" seed="1" labels="yes">
                </network>
              </coordinates>
            </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
        <p component="b"><nbsp/>
        </p>
        <p component="a">
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Initial circuit: <m>(0)</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Expand: <m>(0,6,5,1,4,3,5,8,7,2,11,6,10,12,8,11,7,13,1,9,0)</m>
              </p>
              <p>
                Now <m>11</m> is the first vertex with unused edges.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Walk from <m>11</m>: <m>(11,9,12,11)</m>
              </p>
              <p>
                Splice in: <m>(0,6,5,1,4,3,5,8,7,2,11,9,12,11,6,10,12,8,11,7,13,1,9,0)</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </sidebyside>
      <activity>
        <title>Eulerian graphs</title>
        
        
        
