    <p>
      <alert>Peer instruction questions 1<ndash/>5.</alert>
    </p>
    <theorem xml:id="thm_graphs_dirac">
      <title>Dirac's Theorem</title>
      
      
    <statement>
      <p>If <m>\bfG</m> is a graph on <m>n\geq 3</m> vertices and each vertex in <m>\bfG</m> has at least <m>\lceil
        \frac{n}{2}\rceil</m> neighbors, then <m>\bfG</m> is hamiltonian. </p>
    </statement>
    </theorem>
    <p component="a">
      Notice that this is <em>not</em> and if and only if theorem! Consider the graph <m>\bfC_{10}</m>, in which every vertex has degree <m>2\lt \lceil 10/2\rceil = 5</m>. However, this graph <em>is</em> hamiltonian. Thus, Dirac's theorem allows us to say that <em>some</em> graphs <em>are</em> hamiltonian, but it does not contain the power to say that a graph is <em>not</em> hamiltonian.
    </p>

  </handout>
  
  <handout component="coloring" xml:id="notes-graphs-coloring">
    <title>Graph Coloring</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Peer instruction questions 1<ndash/>3.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      Let <m>\bfG=(V,E)</m> be a graph. Then <m>\phi\colon V\to C</m> is a proper coloring of <m>\bfG</m> if
      <ol marker="A.">
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\phi</m> is one-to-one
          </p>
          <p component="a">
            This means that if <m>x\neq y</m>, then <m>\phi(x)\neq \phi(y)</m>, which would require that all vertices have different colors. This is not required for a proper coloring in most graphs.
          </p>
        </li>
        
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>xy\in E</m> implies <m>\phi(x)\neq \phi(y)</m>
          </p>
          <p component="a">
            Endpoints of edges get different colors.
          </p>
        </li>
        
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\phi</m> uses as few colors from <m>C</m> as possible
          </p>
          <p component="a">
            While this is part of determining the chromatic number, it is not a requirement for a proper coloring.
          </p>
        </li>
        
        <li>
          <p>
            None of the above
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
    
    <p>
      Let <m>\bfG=(V,E)</m> be a graph and <m>\phi\colon V\to C</m> be a proper coloring of <m>\bfG</m>. Let <m>S</m> be all vertices colored <m>1\in C</m>. How many edges does the subgraph of <m>\bfG</m> induced by <m>S</m> contain?
      <ol marker="A.">
        <li>
          <p>
            0
          </p>
        </li>
        
        <li>
          <p>
            1
          </p>
        </li>
        
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>|S|</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        
        <li>
          <p>
            Any number is possible.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
    <p component="a">
      an induced subgraph takes all edges of <m>\bfG</m> with both endpoints in <m>S</m>. Because all vertices of <m>S</m> have the same color and <m>\phi</m> is proper, there can't be any edges. We can also say that <m>S</m> is an <term>independent set</term>.
    </p>
    <p workspace="1cm">
      What is the chromatic number of the complete graph on <m>n</m> vertices <m>\bfK_{n}</m>?
      <ol marker="A.">
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>n-1</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>n</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>n+1</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        
        <li>
          <p>
            There is no fixed formula depending on <m>n</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
    <p component="a">
      Recall that <m>\bfK_n</m> is the <m>n</m>-vertex graph with all possible edges. The <term>chromatic number</term> <m>\chi(\bfG)</m> of a graph <m>\bfG</m> is the smallest number of colors that can be used in a proper coloring of <m>\bfG</m>.
    </p>
    <sidebyside widths="60% 40%">
      <image>
        <description component="a">
          <p>
            A graph with 11 vertices. There is a vertex at the top with label <m>z</m>. Next to this vertex is the number <m>1</m>. There are five edges from <m>z</m>. The neighbors of <m>z</m> each have a label inside the node as well as an integer next to the node. The labels and associated integers are <m>y_1</m> (2), <m>y_2</m> (3), <m>y_3</m> (4), <m>y_4</m> (2), and <m>y_5</m> (5). In addition to the edge to <m>z</m>, each <m>y_i</m> has an edge to two vertices labeled <m>x_{i-1}</m> and <m>x_{i+1}</m>, with subscripts interpreted cyclically so that <m>y_1</m> has an edge to <m>x_5</m> and <m>y_5</m> has an edge to <m>x_1</m>. The <m>x_i</m> also have associated integers, given in order of increasing vertex subscript as <m>4,1,5,3,1</m>. Each <m>x_i</m> has an edge to <m>x_{i+1}</m>, interpreted cyclically so that <m>x_5</m> has an edge to <m>x_1</m>.
          </p>
        </description>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="mycielski-bad-coloring">
          <diagram dimensions="(400,300)" margins="20">
            <coordinates bbox="(0,-0.3,4.2,2)">
              <network labels="yes" node-fill="yellow">
                <node at="1" p="(2,2)" edges="[2,3,4,5,6]"><m>z</m></node>
                <node at="2" p="(0,1)" edges="[8,11]"><m>y_1</m></node>
                <node at="3" p="(1,1)" edges="[7,9]"><m>y_2</m></node>
                <node at="4" p="(2,1)" edges="[8,10]"><m>y_3</m></node>
                <node at="5" p="(3,1)" edges="[9,11]"><m>y_4</m></node>
                <node at="6" p="(4,1)" edges="[7,10]"><m>y_5</m></node>
                <node at="7" p="(0,-0.15)" edges="[8]"><m>x_1</m></node>
                <node at="8" p="(1,0)" edges="[9]"><m>x_2</m></node>
                <node at="9" p="(2,0)" edges="[10]"><m>x_3</m></node>
                <node at="10" p="(3,0)" edges="[11]"><m>x_4</m></node>
                <node at="11" p="(4,-0.15)"><m>x_5</m></node>
                <edge vertices="(7,11)"/>
              </network>
              <label p="(1.87,2)" alignment="w"><m>1</m></label>
              <label p="(0.2,1)" alignment="e"><m>2</m></label>
              <label p="(1.1,1)" alignment="e"><m>3</m></label>
              <label p="(2.1,1)" alignment="e"><m>4</m></label>
              <label p="(3.1,1)" alignment="e"><m>2</m></label>
              <label p="(4.1,1)" alignment="e"><m>5</m></label>
              <label p="(0,-.25)" alignment="s"><m>4</m></label>
              <label p="(1.1,0)" alignment="ne"><m>1</m></label>
              <label p="(2.1,0)" alignment="ne"><m>5</m></label>
              <label p="(2.9,0)" alignment="nw"><m>3</m></label>
              <label p="(4,-0.25)" alignment="s"><m>1</m></label>
            </coordinates>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
      <p component="b"><nbsp/></p>
      <p component="a">
        The given proper <m>5</m>-coloring shows that <m>\chi(\bfG)\leq 5</m>. However, there are several ways to recolor this graph using four colors. One example would involve changing the color of <m>y_5</m> to be <m>3</m> and then recoloring each <m>x_i</m> to have the same color as <m>y_i</m> has under the modified coloring. Notice that this graph does not contain any subgraphs isomorphic to <m>\bfK_3</m>, which we usually call a <term>triangle</term>.
      </p>
    </sidebyside>
    <definition workspace="4cm">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A graph is <term>bipartite</term> provided that its chromatic number is <m>2</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p component="a">
      We can visualize a bipartite graph as one in which the vertex set can be partitioned into two sets <m>V=X\cup Y</m> so that every edge has one endpoint in <m>X</m> and one endpoint in <m>Y</m>. The <term>complete bipartite graph</term> <m>\bfK_{m,n}</m> is formed in this manner by placing <m>m</m> vertices in <m>X</m> and <m>n</m> vertices in <m>Y</m> and then putting all possible edges between vertices in <m>X</m> and vertices in <m>Y</m>.
    </p>
    <theorem workspace="4cm">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A graph is bipartite if and only if it does not contain any odd cycles.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>  
    <p component="a">
      Because this is an if and only if theorem, we know that if <m>\bfG</m> contains an odd cycle, then <m>\chi(\bfG)\geq 3</m>. For efficiently finding a 2-coloring or an odd cycle, pick a vertex and start coloring it 1. Then color its neighbors 2. Then color their neighbors 1. Continue. Either you will end up with a vertex that needs to be both 1 and 2, in which you can trace back an odd cycle, or you will get a proper 2-coloring. (Proceed component-wise if the graph is not connected.)
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Peer instruction question 4.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert component="b">First Fit</alert>
      <alert component="a">First Fit also known as the Greedy Algorithm</alert>
    </p>
      
      <sidebyside widths="20% 20%">
        <image>
          <description component="a">
            <p>
              A graph with 8 vertices. The vertices are arranged in two columns of four vertices. There are no edges between vertices in the same column. Each vertex has three edges, which go to the three vertices in the other column that are not in the same row as that vertex. The vertices in the left column are labeled from top to bottom as <m>v_1,v_3,v_5,v_7</m>. The vertices in the right column are labeled from top to bottom as <m>v_2,v_4,v_6,v_8</m>.
            </p>
          </description>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="K44-M-bad-order">
            <diagram dimensions="(100,150)" margins="15">
              <coordinates bbox="(0,0,1,3)">
                <network labels="yes" node-fill="yellow">
                  <node at="1" p="(0,3)" edges="[6,7,8]"><m>v_1</m></node>
                  <node at="2" p="(0,2)" edges="[5,7,8]"><m>v_3</m></node>
                  <node at="3" p="(0,1)" edges="[5,6,8]"><m>v_5</m></node>
                  <node at="4" p="(0,0)" edges="[5,6,7]"><m>v_7</m></node>
                  <node at="5" p="(1,3)"><m>v_2</m></node>
                  <node at="6" p="(1,2)"><m>v_4</m></node>
                  <node at="7" p="(1,1)"><m>v_6</m></node>
                  <node at="8" p="(1,0)"><m>v_8</m></node>
                </network>
              </coordinates>
            </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
        <image>
          <description component="a">
            <p>
              A graph with 8 vertices. The vertices are arranged in two columns of four vertices. There are no edges between vertices in the same column. Each vertex has three edges, which go to the three vertices in the other column that are not in the same row as that vertex. The vertices in the left column are labeled from top to bottom as <m>v_1,v_2,v_3,v_4</m>. The vertices in the right column are labeled from top to bottom as <m>v_5,v_6,v_7,v_8</m>.
            </p>
          </description>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="K44-M-good-order">
            <diagram dimensions="(100,150)" margins="15">
              <coordinates bbox="(0,0,1,3)">
                <network labels="yes" node-fill="yellow">
                  <node at="1" p="(0,3)" edges="[6,7,8]"><m>v_1</m></node>
                  <node at="2" p="(0,2)" edges="[5,7,8]"><m>v_2</m></node>
                  <node at="3" p="(0,1)" edges="[5,6,8]"><m>v_3</m></node>
                  <node at="4" p="(0,0)" edges="[5,6,7]"><m>v_4</m></node>
                  <node at="5" p="(1,3)"><m>v_5</m></node>
                  <node at="6" p="(1,2)"><m>v_6</m></node>
                  <node at="7" p="(1,1)"><m>v_7</m></node>
                  <node at="8" p="(1,0)"><m>v_8</m></node>
                </network>
              </coordinates>
            </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>    
      </sidebyside>
      <p component="a">
        First Fit uses 4 colors on the left graph because it colors each row of vertices (in the context of the graph as drawn) with the same color. Using the ordering on the left, the vertices in the left column are all colored 1 and those in the right column are all colored 2. This generalizes quite directly to a similar graph in which there are <m>n</m> vertices in each column, requiring <m>n</m> colors with the ordering on the left but still only 2 colors with the ordering on the right.
      </p>
  </handout>
  
  
  <handout xml:id="planar" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Planar Graphs</title>
    <page>
        <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            A <term>drawing</term> of a graph is a way of associating the graph's vertices with points in the plane and its edges with simple polygonal arcs between the points corresponding to the edge's endpoints.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A <term>planar drawing</term> of a graph is one in which the polygonal arcs corresponding to edges intersect only at the points corresponding to the endpoints of the appropriate edges.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A graph is <term>planar</term> if it has a planar drawing.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <tabular>
      <row bottom="minor" header="yes">
        <cell>Polyhedron</cell>
        <cell>Vertices <m>n</m></cell>
        <cell>Edges <m>m</m></cell>
        <cell>Faces <m>f</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Tetrahedron</cell>
        <cell component="a">4</cell>
        <cell component="a">6</cell>
        <cell component="a">4</cell>
      </row>
      <row component="b">
        <cell></cell>
        <cell></cell>
        <cell></cell>
        <cell></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Octahedron</cell>
        <cell component="a">6</cell>
        <cell component="a">12</cell>
        <cell component="a">8</cell>
      </row>
      <row component="b">
        <cell></cell>
        <cell></cell>
        <cell></cell>
        <cell></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Cube</cell>
        <cell component="a">8</cell>
        <cell component="a">12</cell>
        <cell component="a">6</cell>
      </row>
      <row component="b">
        <cell></cell>
        <cell></cell>
        <cell></cell>
        <cell></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Icosahedron</cell>
        <cell component="a">12</cell>
        <cell component="a">30</cell>
        <cell component="a">20</cell>
      </row>
      <row component="b">
        <cell></cell>
        <cell></cell>
        <cell></cell>
        <cell></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Dodecahedron</cell>
        <cell component="a">20</cell>
        <cell component="a">30</cell>
        <cell component="a">12</cell>
      </row>
      <row component="b">
        <cell></cell>
        <cell></cell>
        <cell></cell>
        <cell></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
    <p component="a">
      We conjecture the formula
      <md>
        n-m+f = 2
      </md>
      and will sketch a proof through the activity.
    </p>
    <p workspace="0.5cm">
      
    </p>
    <sidebyside widths="48% 48%">
      <image source="5-5_math_art_expanded.jpg">
        <shortdescription>The plane of polyhedra</shortdescription>
      </image>
      <image source="5-5_math_art_detail.jpg">
        <shortdescription>Polyhedra zoomed in so that axis scales are visible</shortdescription>
      </image>
      
    </sidebyside>
  <p component="b" workspace="0.1cm">
    
  </p>
  </page>
    <page>
    <p component="a">
      There are two common drawings of <m>\bfK_4</m>, only one of which is planar. The existence of this planar drawing tells us that <m>\bfK_4</m> is planar. Keep in mind that there is always an unbounded face when we count faces in a planar drawing!
    </p>
    <p workspace="2cm">
      
    </p>
    <p>
      Takeaways from the handout:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p component="a">
            If <m>\bfG</m> is planar, then <m>m\leq 3n-6</m>. This is more useful in the form <q>If <m>m\gt 3n-6</m>, then <m>\bfG</m> is not planar.</q>
          </p>
          <p component="b" workspace="1cm">
            
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p component="a">
            Corollary: <m>\bfK_5</m> is not planar.
          </p>
          <p component="b" workspace="1cm">
            
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p component="a">
            <m>\bfK_{3,3}</m> is not planar.
          </p>
          <p component="b" workspace="1cm">
            
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-kuratowski">
      <title>Kuratowski's Theorem</title>
      
      
      <statement>
        <p>
          A graph <m>\bfG</m> is planar if and only if it does not contain a subdivision of <m>\bfK_5</m> or <m>\bfK_{3,3}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>
    <p component="a">
      A subdivision is formed by replacing one or more edges of <m>\bfG</m> by <em>disjoint</em> paths. Morally, the only nonplanar graphs contain <m>\bfK_5</m> and <m>\bfK_{3,3}</m>.
    </p>
    <p component="b" workspace="3cm">
      
    </p>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-four-color">
      <title>The Four Color Theorem</title>
      
      
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>\bfG</m> is a planar graph, then <m>\chi(\bfG)\leq 4</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>
    <p component="a">
      This theorem was <q>proved</q> many times over the years, but all the proofs were flawed until Appel and Haken provided a computer-assisted proof. This was the first computer-assisted proof that could not be readily converted to a pencil and paper proof that ended up being widely accepted. In the early 1990's, a computer assisted proof with fewer cases was provided, but to this day, no one knows a proof that does not rely on computers.
    </p>
  </page>
  </handout>
  <handout component="prufer" label="graphs-prufer">
    <title>Counting Labeled Trees</title>
    <page>
      <p workspace="2in">
        <alert>Labeled vs Unlabeled Trees (and Graphs) and Counting</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Big Idea</alert>
      </p>
        <sidebyside widths="60% 40%">
          <image>
            <description component="a">
              <p>
                A diagram containing two transparent ellipses, stacked vertically.   
              </p>
            </description>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="ellipses-prufer">
              <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
                <coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,4,4)">
                  <ellipse center="(0,2.25)" axes="(3,1.25)" stroke="black" fill="#1E88E5" opacity="0.15"/>
                  <ellipse center="(0,-2.25)" axes="(3,1.25)" stroke="black" fill="#FFC107" opacity="0.15"/>
                </coordinates>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
          <p>
            <nbsp/>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>
      
    </page>
    <page>
      
        <algorithm>
          <title>Labeled Tree <m>\longrightarrow</m> Prüfer Code</title>
          
          
        <p>

          Assume <m>\bfT</m> is a labeled tree with at least <m>2</m> vertices.
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>\prufer(\bfT)</m> is defined recursively by
          <ol marker="1.">
            <li>
              <p>
                If <m>\bfT\cong \bfK_{2}</m>, return the empty string.
              </p>
            </li>
            
            <li>
              <p>
                Else, let <m>v</m> be the leaf of <m>\bfT</m> with the smallest label and let <m>u</m> be its unique neighbor.
                Let <m>i</m> be the label of <m>u</m>.
                Return <m>(i,\prufer(\bfT-v))</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
        </algorithm>
        <p>
          <alert>Peer instruction question 1 followed by Activity 5.5.1.</alert>
        </p>
      
      <p>
        <alert>Peer instruction questions 2<ndash/>3.</alert>
      </p>
      <algorithm>
        <title>Prüfer Code <m>\longrightarrow</m> Labeled Tree</title>
        <p>
          <ol marker="1.">
            <li>
              <p>
                Keep track of three things
                <ul>
                  <li>
                    <p>
                      Prüfer code
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  
                  <li>
                    <p>
                      Labels remaining
                    </p>
                  </li>
                  
                  <li>
                    <p>
                      Edge added
                    </p>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </p>
            </li>
            
            <li>
              <p>
                Smallest remaining label not in code and first label of code determine edge to add
              </p>
            </li>
            
            <li>
              <p>
                Remove first entry of Prüfer code.
                Remove label just used from label set.
              </p>
            </li>
            
            <li>
              <p>
                Repeat until Prüfer code is empty (remaining labels are edge).
              </p>
            </li>
            
            <li>
              <p>
                Build tree reading edges from bottom to top.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
        </algorithm>
        <p>
          <alert>Peer Instruction Question 4.</alert>
        </p>
        
        <tabular component="b">
        <col halign="center" right="minor"> </col> <col halign="center" right="minor"> </col> <col halign="center"> </col>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Prüfer code</cell>
          <cell>Label set</cell>
          <cell>Edge added</cell>
        </row>
        
        <row>
          <cell>8431875</cell>
          <cell> <m>\{1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9\}</m> </cell>
          <cell>  </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>\phantom{123456789123456789}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\phantom{1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9,1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9}</m></cell>
          <cell><nbsp/></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell><nbsp/></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell><nbsp/></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell><nbsp/></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell><nbsp/></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell><nbsp/></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell><nbsp/></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell><nbsp/></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell><nbsp/></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell><nbsp/></cell>
        </row>
        </tabular>
      
    </page>
    <page>
      <tabular>
        <col halign="center" right="minor"> </col> <col halign="center" right="minor"> </col> <col halign="center"> </col>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Prüfer code</cell>
          <cell>Label set</cell>
          <cell>Edge added</cell>
        </row>
        
        <row>
          <cell>8431875</cell>
          <cell> <m>\{1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9\}</m> </cell>
          <cell> <m>2-8</m> </cell>
        </row>
        
        <row>
          <cell>431875</cell>
          <cell> <m>\{1,3,4,5,6,7,8,9\}</m> </cell>
          <cell> <m>6-4</m> </cell>
        </row>
        
        <row>
